nt to align on to this  one NBF the last thing that i brought in  and so we'll use the super command and  that popped in right there we can zoom  in and see that ubiquitin is aligned  fairly well and now let's go ahead and  try to superimpose the protein that's  unbound to the one that is bound to  ubiquitin so we'll just replace that  first part with one nb8 and this jumps  over here and we can see that this  really is an aligning well we have an  alpha helix there's nothing there these  beta sheets are kind of intertwined so  it's a really good thing to check our  sequence at this point and make sure of  course you want the sequence to be the  same from the same organism and we can  see that this sequence here is aligned  is matched up pretty well let's say so  when this sort of thing happens you know  it might just be the conformational  change is so great that pi mole doesn't  know what to do perhaps it's choosing a  different part of the protein notice  this one is much bigger on the the pink  one here one and bf so it has a lot more  going on in the structure so maybe it  doesn't know where to align it very well  so what we can do is create a selection  so I'm gonna take this chain a and I'm  gonna scroll over and then I see this  part where some residues are missing the  crystallographer knows they're there but  they couldn't actually see them so I'm  just gonna go up to that point so I'll  press shift and click so I can select  that whole part and now I'm going to  rename my selection so we'll call this  one n b8 SEL e  and that'll pop up here now I'm going to  click elsewhere start at the beginning  here again and just go up to the same  point and make a selection  now we'll rename the selection to one  and be f SE le and there we have it now  we're going to reapply our super command  but using the selections that we made so  all I have to add on here is se le se le  and look at that that's a lot better now  we have a lot of parts of this protein  that aren